## Lesson 63 – The Threshold

Every BASIC project hits a ceiling. This lesson builds a tiny performance monitor that watches frame time, scales back optional effects, and prints a “next steps” report. It’s your gut-check before committing to assembly.

> [📷 suggested: screenshot showing frame budget readout and toggled features]

---

### The One-Minute Tour

- Measure frame length with `TI`; compare it to your target budget (e.g., 1/60th of a second ≈ 1 jiffy).
- Keep a list of features with priorities—disable the lowest priority first when you miss budget.
- Log the disabled features so you know what to rewrite in machine code later.

---

### Example Program

```basic
NEW
10 REM *** PERFORMANCE THRESHOLD TEST ***
20 PRINT CHR$(147);"FRAME BUDGET MONITOR"
30 TARGET=3:REM 3 jiffies (~1/20th second)
40 DIM FEATURE$(3),ACTIVE(3)
50 DATA "STARFIELD",1,"SPARKS",1,"WAVEFORM",1
60 FOR I=0 TO 2:READ FEATURE$(I):READ ACTIVE(I):NEXT
70 PRINT "PRESS SPACE TO RUN, RETURN TO EXIT"
80 GET K$:IF K$=CHR$(13) THEN END
90 IF K$<>" " THEN 80
100 PRINT CHR$(147)
110 TI$="000000"
120 GOSUB 400:REM run frame
130 FRAME=TI
140 PRINT "FRAME:";FRAME;" jiffies"
150 IF FRAME>TARGET THEN GOSUB 500
160 GOSUB 600
170 IF NOTREADY=3 THEN PRINT "ALL OPTIONAL FEATURES DISABLED" :END
180 GOTO 110

400 REM *** SIMULATED WORKLOAD ***
410 IF ACTIVE(0) THEN FOR I=0 TO 150:NEXT
420 IF ACTIVE(1) THEN FOR I=0 TO 50:Z=SIN(I):NEXT
430 IF ACTIVE(2) THEN FOR I=0 TO 20:POKE 54276,33:POKE 54296,0:NEXT
440 RETURN

500 REM *** DEGRADATION MANAGER ***
510 FOR I=2 TO 0 STEP -1
520 IF ACTIVE(I) THEN ACTIVE(I)=0:PRINT "DISABLING ";FEATURE$(I):RETURN
530 NEXT I
540 RETURN

600 REM *** STATUS HUD ***
610 PRINT "ACTIVE FEATURES:";
620 NOTREADY=0
630 FOR I=0 TO 2
640 IF ACTIVE(I) THEN PRINT FEATURE$(I);" "; ELSE NOTREADY=NOTREADY+1
650 NEXT I
660 PRINT
670 RETURN
```

Sample output:

```
FRAME: 6 jiffies
DISABLING WAVEFORM
ACTIVE FEATURES: STARFIELD SPARKS
FRAME: 4 jiffies
DISABLING SPARKS
ACTIVE FEATURES: STARFIELD
FRAME: 1 jiffies
```

The workload simulates three optional systems. When the frame time exceeds the target (here 3 jiffies), we disable the lowest-priority feature. Once performance recovers, the HUD shows what’s left. In a real game you’d swap the loops for your starfield draw, particle effects, or SID modulation.

> **Tip:** Don’t wait for players to complain—run this monitor during development to see which features hurt first.

---

### Experiment Section

- Move the features into actual library calls: starfield from Week 7, machine-code colour cycler from Lesson 61, etc.
- Change the priority order to match your game—maybe you’d rather keep audio than particles.
- Add a log to disk so you have a list of candidates for machine-code rewrites.
- Print a warning when all options are disabled so you know you’ve hit BASIC’s limit.

> [🎥 suggested: capture showing frame time dropping as features disable]

---

### Concept Expansion

This pattern scales: keep optional systems in an array, disable them when the interpreter can’t keep up, and record which ones fell off. Those items become your to-do list for the Transition course. Instead of guessing the threshold, you measured it.

---

### Game Integration

Drop into your main loop:

```basic
FRAME=TI
IF FRAME>FRAME_TARGET THEN GOSUB 4200
```

Keep the cleanup routine parked at line `4200` (tagged with a `REM DROP OPTIONAL FEATURE`). When the array empties, you know it’s time to port the heaviest logic to assembly.

